{At board no.1, Mr WV Naish, the Cambridge captain, was opposed by Mr E Lawton, captain of the Oxford team. Mr Lawton adopted a French defence, which he conducted up to the sixth move as in his game against Mr Griffith [1 e4 e6 2 d4 d5 3 Nc3 Nf6 4 e5 Nfd7 5 f4 c5 6 dxc5 Nxc5 - BCM, May 1896, p195]. On the 7th move Mr Naish varied and a set up a premature attack by 9 Q-Kt4 and came out a P minus.} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. dxc5 Nxc5 7. Be3 Nc6 8. Bxc5 Bxc5 9. Qg4 g6 10. Nf3 Qa5 11. O-O-O d4 12. Ne4 Qxa2 13. Nxc5 Qa1+ 14. Kd2 Qa5+ 15. Kc1 Qxc5 16. Ne1 Bd7 17. Nd3 Qb6 18. Qh4 a5 19. Kd2 Nb4 20. Nxb4 Qxb4+ 21. Kc1 a4 22. Rd3 a3 23. Rxa3 Rxa3 24. bxa3 Qxa3+ 25. Kd2 Qc3+ 26. Kd1 Ba4 27. Qf2 Qa1+ 28. Kd2 Qb2 29. Kd1 Qb1+ 30. Ke2 Qxc2+ 31. Kf3 Qxf2+ 32. Kxf2 Bc2 33. Bb5+ Ke7 34. Rc1 d3 35. Ke3 Rd8 36. Kd2 Rd5 {Sources: London Evening Standard, 1 April 1896; The Field, 4 April 1896} 0-1

