{At board no.2, Mr Varley (Cambridge) also adopted a French Defence, his opponent being Mr Spencer Churchill (Oxford). White also in this game attacked by 7 Q-Kt4, but with prospects than at board no.1, whilst Black weakened his KP by advancing 8 P-KB4 and this was a source of subsequent trouble to the end of the game... the game was left for adjudication, the adjudicators giving it as a win for White. Source: BCM, May 1896, p197} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. dxc5 Bxc5 7. Qg4 O-O 8. Nf3 f5 9. Qg3 h6 10. Bd2 a6 11. Bd3 Rf7 12. Ne2 Be7 13. c4 Nf8 14. Rd1 dxc4 15. Bxc4 b5 16. Bb3 Bb7 17. O-O Qb6+ 18. Kh1 Nbd7 19. Nfd4 Nc5 20. Bc2 Be4 21. Be3 Qb7 22. Bxe4 Nxe4 23. Qf3 Qc8 24. Ng3 Nxg3+ 25. hxg3 Rb8 26. Rc1 Qe8 27. Qc6 Bb4 28. a3 Ba5 29. b4 Bb6 30. Qxe8 Rxe8 31. Rc6 Bxd4 32. Bxd4 Ra8 33. Rfc1 Rd7 34. Rd6 Kf7 35. Bc5 Rxd6 36. Bxd6 Nd7 37. Rc7 Ke8 38. Kh2 Kd8 39. Kh3 a5 40. Rb7 axb4 41. axb4 Ra6 42. Rxb5 Rb6 {adjudicated a win by Hoffer and Lasker. Sources: London Evening Standard, 1 April 1896; The Field, 4 April 1896} 1-0

