{"Board No.3 was the first game concluded. R. G. Cooper [sic] (Oxford) defended the Centre Gambit with 4...P to Q Kt3, an inferior defence to start with, and he followed it up with an unsound sacrifice on the 11th move. W. T. Quin (Cambridge) took vigorous and decisive advantage of his opponent's exposed King's position, and on the 18th move he was a whole Rook ahead, winning the game in four more moves. Mr Cooper, who had shown considerable talent as a Freshman three years ago, has been out of practice of late." (London Evening Standard - Wednesday 01 April 1896) CONJECTURE - The Centre Gambit is 1 e4 e5 2 d4 exd4 3 Bc4 according to an article in BCM, August 1898, p324. The term is no longer used, but the game MIGHT have begun:} 1. e4 e5 2. d4 exd4 3. Bc4 {then perhaps} ({Or perhaps} 3. c3 dxc3 4. Nxc3 b6) 3... Nc6 ({Did Black perhaps play} 3... b6 {on the 3rd move?}) 4. Nf3 b6 {... 22 moves (Source: London Evening Standard, 1 April 1896)} 1-0

