1. e4 e5 2. d4 exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 4. Qe3 Nf6 5. Bd2 d6 6. Nc3 Be7 7. h3 O-O 8. O-O-O Nd7 9. g4 Nc5 10. f4 Bf6 11. f5 Bd4 12. Qe1 Bxc3 13. Bxc3 Na4 14. Ne2 Nxc3 15. Nxc3 Ne5 16. Qg3 Bd7 17. h4 f6 18. g5 Bc6 19. Bg2 b5 20. Rhg1 b4 21. Ne2 a5 22. Nf4 Qe7 23. Ne6 Rfc8 24. Bh1 fxg5 25. Qxg5 Qxg5+ 26. Rxg5 g6 27. fxg6 hxg6 28. Nf4 Kh7 29. Rdg1 Rg8 30. h5 Be8 31. Nd5 Rg7 32. Nf4 Bf7 33. hxg6+ Bxg6 34. Nxg6 Rxg6 35. Rxg6 Nxg6 36. e5 Rd8 37. Be4 Rg8 {White lost on time. Sources: London Evening Standard, 1 April 1896; The Field, 4 April 1896. Played at the British Chess Club. For Black's 37th move the score does not tell us whether Black moved his rook to QN1 or KN1 (Brian Denman) (Also, BCM: "White lost on time. BCM, May 1896, page 197-8 commented: "At board no.4, another Centre Gambit was played, Mr. H. N. Robbins (Oxford) having the attack, and Mr. E. A. Crowley (Cambridge) the defence. White castled on Q side, and pushing his P's on the K's flank, got a very strong attack, which he somewhat weakened, however, by exchanging Q's 5, and drifted into an end-game, which he lost by exceeding his time-limit. White's last move was R-Kt square.")} 0-1

