{At board no. 5, Mr R. A. Jenkins (Oxford) defended with a Queen's fianchetto (1...b6, confirmed by London Evening Standard report) against Mr R. Battersby (Cambridge). The Cantab failed to get the most out of the opening, and subsequently sacrificed the exchange, in the hope of obtaining an attack. We annex a diagram of the position after White's 38th move (B-K5). The game went on:} 38... g5 39. Qg3 Rg6 40. Rf1 f6 41. Bb8 {and after a few more moves the game was adjudicated a win for Black. (Sources: BCM, May 1896, p198; The Field, 4 April 1896; London Evening Standard, 1 April 1896)} 0-1

