{At board No. 6, Mr. H. F. Parker (Oxford) defended with a Petroff against Mr. G. Fraser (Cam­bridge), but the game soon passed into a form of the Giuoco Piano, in which Black got somewhat the better game, and what, at one time, looked like a promising attack, but a weak move or two lost him the exchange, though he had Pawns in hand. At the 29th move the game was given up as a draw, in the position shown on the diagram. (BCM, May 1896, p193)} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. Bc4 Bc5 5. d3 d6 6. Bg5 Be6 7. Bxe6 fxe6 8. Ne2 O-O 9. Ng3 Qd7 10. h3 Nd4 11. c3 Nxf3+ 12. gxf3 Rf7 13. d4 exd4 14. cxd4 Bb6 15. Qd3 Raf8 16. O-O-O Ne8 17. Be3 Rxf3 18. Rdg1 c6 19. d5 e5 20. Nf5 Kh8 21. Qe2 Bxe3+ 22. fxe3 R3xf5 23. exf5 cxd5 24. Qg4 Rxf5 25. Rf1 g6 26. Rxf5 gxf5 27. Qg3 Ng7 28. Qg2 e4 29. Qd2 {Sources: London Evening Standard, 1 April 1896; The Field, 4 April 1896} 1/2-1/2

